<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="52000" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN/529/52000.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="CMN" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="529" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/CMN/529.xml">Seminar Communication Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>JY </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>AI &amp; Computational Comm</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This graduate seminar is a hybrid course that covers communication theories, computational methods, and the application of generative AI. Students will review classic literature in communication and social science that employed 'traditional' research methods, learn various computational social science techniques, and re-examine foundational theories using new data, methodologies, and generative AI tools. Intermediate knowledge in R is required.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="S3 ">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>M      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4103</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Yang" firstName="J">Yang, J</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>